commit 38d14938da9cd58fc1e693229ba8a03813f3e147
Author: Mauro Barchiesi <139244198+MBark286@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Oct 7 18:18:25 2026 +0200

    MLXEmbedders: use bidirectional attention in EmbeddingGemma (#654)
    
    * MLXEmbedders: add createBidirectionalAttentionMask
    
    Encoder models need a bidirectional attention mask with an optional
    symmetric window and key padding. The existing helpers do not fit:
    createAttentionMask is causal, and createBidirectionalMask and
    createBidirectionalSlidingWindowMask in MLXLMCommon build additive masks
    with no padding and a window on the absolute key position.
    
    createBidirectionalAttentionMask(length:halfWindow:paddingMask:) returns
    .none when halfWindow and paddingMask are both nil. Otherwise it returns
    a boolean [batch or 1, 1, length, length] mask where query i may attend
    key j when |i - j| <= halfWindow and key j is not padding, or when
    i == j.
    
    - Padding hides key columns only, as in transformers, so a padded query
      row still sees the real keys. The boolean key mask follows Qwen3.
    - The diagonal is always kept, so no row is fully masked. For a fully
      masked row, MLX's attention returns a uniform average, zeros or
      another finite value depending on the kernel path.
    - A window that covers the whole sequence skips the band, so a huge
      halfWindow never reaches Int32.
    
    Tests cover the inclusive band bound, a zero window, padding on columns,
    padding dtypes, a single token, and agreement on real rows with the
    transformers 5.17 Gemma 3 sliding-window rule for windows 7, 8 and 9.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * MLXEmbedders: use bidirectional attention in EmbeddingGemma
    
    EmbeddingGemma is a bidirectional encoder and its config.json says so
    ("use_bidirectional_attention": true), but the Swift backbone ignored
    the key and built causal masks, so every token saw only the tokens
    before it. Against transformers 5.17 with the original
    google/embeddinggemma-300m weights, the final embeddings had a cosine
    of only 0.45 to 0.70.
    
    Gemma3Configuration now decodes use_bidirectional_attention; absent or
    null means false. When it is true:
    
    - Sliding layers see sliding_window / 2 tokens on each side. This is
      the band transformers uses: it stores sliding_window // 2 + 1 and
      tests |i - j| < it, so 512 gives |i - j| <= 256.
    - Global layers see every token.
    - The padding mask passed to EmbeddingGemma.callAsFunction reaches the
      backbone and hides padded keys on every layer, so a padded batch
      gives the same states as its texts run one at a time. A 1-D mask is
      accepted with a 1-D input, and the backbone checks that the mask and
      the inputs have the same shape.
    
    Without a mask, attention sees no padding, as in transformers; padded
    batches need attentionMask. When the flag is false or absent the masks
    are the causal ones as before, so gemma3 and gemma3_text checkpoints
    that are causal by design do not change.
    
    Tests cover the flag-false path (padding ignored, causal, causal
    window), config decoding, bidirectionality in both directions, the
    window bound for sliding_window 8 and 9, right, left and mixed-length
    padded batches against single texts, 1-D inputs, and bfloat16 weights
    with padding.
    
    Fixes #638
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/Libraries/MLXEmbedders/AttentionMask.swift b/Libraries/MLXEmbedders/AttentionMask.swift
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..00b4a63
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Libraries/MLXEmbedders/AttentionMask.swift
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+// Copyright © 2026 Apple Inc.
+
+import MLX
+
+/// Builds a bidirectional attention mask for encoder models.
+///
+/// Every query attends to keys within `halfWindow` positions on either side
+/// (inclusive). With `halfWindow == nil` it attends to all keys. Zero entries
+/// of `paddingMask` (`[batch, length]`) mask out the matching key columns.
+/// The diagonal is always kept so that no row is fully masked.
+///
+/// Returns `.none` when `halfWindow` and `paddingMask` are both nil; otherwise a boolean
+/// array of shape `[batch or 1, 1, length, length]`.
+///
+/// - Precondition: `length > 0`, `halfWindow >= 0` when given, and `paddingMask`
+///   has shape `[batch, length]`.
+func createBidirectionalAttentionMask(
+    length: Int,
+    halfWindow: Int?,
+    paddingMask: MLXArray?
+) -> MLXFast.ScaledDotProductAttentionMaskMode {
+    precondition(length > 0, "length must be positive")
+    if let halfWindow {
+        precondition(halfWindow >= 0, "halfWindow must not be negative")
+    }
+    if let paddingMask {
+        precondition(
+            paddingMask.ndim == 2 && paddingMask.dim(1) == length,
+            "paddingMask must have shape [batch, \(length)]")
+    }
+
+    // Nothing to mask: let attention take the fast path.
+    if halfWindow == nil && paddingMask == nil { return .none }
+
+    var mask: MLXArray
+    if let halfWindow, halfWindow < length - 1 {
+        // Band |i - j| <= halfWindow. A window covering the whole sequence
+        // skips this branch, which also keeps Int.max away from Int32.
+        let positions = MLXArray(0 ..< length)
+        let distance = abs(positions[0..., .newAxis] - positions[.newAxis, 0...])
+        mask = distance .<= MLXArray(Int32(halfWindow))
+    } else {
+        mask = MLXArray.ones([length, length], dtype: .bool)
+    }
+    mask = mask[.newAxis, .newAxis, 0..., 0...]
+
+    if let paddingMask {
+        // Mask key columns, not query rows: every query still sees the valid keys.
+        let validKeys = paddingMask.asType(.bool)[0..., .newAxis, .newAxis, 0...]
+        mask = mask .&& validKeys
+    }
+
+    // Keep the diagonal: a fully masked row gives kernel-dependent results.
+    mask = mask .|| eye(length, type: Bool.self)
+
+    return .array(mask)
+}
