commit 282c2f505a8b42b76b37e192dcc61a4ddaec8d5c
Author: Alessio Pollero <alessio.pollero@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Oct 5 23:37:47 2026 +0200

    Report KV cache memory in KVCacheStatus (#639)
    
    `KVCacheStatus` describes a cache's topology, strategy and progress, but not
    how much memory it holds, so an application cannot show what a conversation
    costs in unified memory. Two cache types count their own bytes
    (`TurboQuantKVCache.memoryBytes` and the internal
    `VarianceNormalizedKVCache.compactStorageByteCount`); the others leave
    callers to guess.
    
    Report it for every layer:
    
    - Add `KVCacheLayerStatus.memoryBytes`, the bytes allocated for the layer's
      arrays, and `KVCacheStatus.memoryBytes`, their total. Rows a cache
      reserves ahead of the tokens it holds are counted, since they are resident.
    - Count them from `innerState()`, the arrays `Evaluatable` already requires
      each cache to list. `nbytes` reads array metadata only, so building a
      status evaluates nothing and never waits on the GPU.
    - `TurboQuantKVCache` inherited `BaseKVCache`'s empty `innerState()`, so it
      reported no bytes and `asyncEval(cache)`, which chunked prefill runs after
      every chunk, skipped its buffers. List them, and count `memoryBytes` from
      the same list.
    - `VarianceNormalizedKVCache.innerState()` returned `state`, which slices a
      view of every tile on each call. Return the slabs, pending tiles and tail
      it stores instead: the same bytes, without work that grows with the
      context.
    
    Statuses are built when a turn starts, never in the token loop, and every
    built-in cache now lists only the arrays it stores, so the count stays
    cheap at any context length.

